==German==

===Alternative forms===
* {{l|de|steh'n}}
* {{l|de|stehn}} {{qualifier|dated in formal prose, but still common informally or poetically}}

===Etymology===
{{root|de|ine-pro|*steh₂-}}
From {{inh|de|gmh|stān}}, {{m|gmh|stēn}}, from {{inh|de|goh|stān}}, {{m|goh|stēn}}, from {{inh|de|gmw-pro|*stān}}, from {{inh|de|gem-pro|*stāną}}. The ''-h-'' was introduced into the spelling by analogy with {{m|de|sehen}}, in which it had become mute but was retained in spelling.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|de|/ˈʃteː.ən/}} {{a|official standard, but less common}}
* {{IPA|de|/ʃteːn/}} {{a|predominant}}
* {{rhymes|de|eːən|eːn}}
* {{hyph|de|ste|hen}}
* {{audio|de|De-stehen.ogg|Audio}}
* {{audio|de|De-at-stehen.ogg|audio (Austria)}}

===Verb===
{{de-verb-irregular|steht|stand|gestanden|auxiliary=both|past subjunctive=[[stände]] or [[stünde]]}}

# {{lb|de|intransitive}} to [[stand]] {{gloss|to be upright, support oneself on the feet in an erect position}}
#: {{ux|de|Ich '''stehe''' an der Ecke.|I'm '''standing''' on the corner.}}
# {{lb|de|intransitive}} to [[be]], to [[appear]], to [[stand]] {{gloss|to be placed or located somewhere}}
#: {{ux|de|Das '''steht''' nicht in dem Wörterbuch.|This does not '''appear''' in the dictionary.}}
#* {{quote-book|de|year=1931|author={{w|Arthur Schnitzler}}|title=Flucht in die Finsternis|url={{fullurl:s:de:Flucht in die Finsternis}}|chapter=IV|chapterurl={{fullurl:s:de:Flucht in die Finsternis/IV}}|publisher=S. Fischer Verlag|page=36|passage=Ein frisch gefülltes Glas Champagner '''stand''' vor ihm. Er trank es in einem Zug aus – mit Lust, fast mit Begier.|translation=A freshly filled glass of champagne '''was''' in front of him. He emptied it in one draught – with pleasure, almost with greed.}}
# {{lb|de|intransitive}} to [[stay]]; to be [[still]]
#: {{uxi|de|Das Leben '''steht'''.|Life '''stands still'''.}}
# {{lb|de|transitive}} to [[suit]], to [[become]] {{gloss|to look attractive on, of a garment, color etc.}}
#: {{uxi|de|Blau '''steht''' dir sehr gut!|t=Blue suits you very well!}}
#: {{uxi|de|Der Tod '''steht''' ihr gut|t=Death '''Becomes''' Her (film title)}}
# {{lb|de|Swiss}} to [[put]], [[place]]
#: {{syn|de|[[stellen|sich stellen]]}}
# {{indtr|de|auf|.colloquial}} to [[fancy]]
#: {{ux|de|Ich glaube sie '''steht''' nicht auf dich.|I don't think she '''fancies''' you.}}
#* {{quote-song|de||title=Wir Steh'n Auf Berlin|artist=Ideal|year=1980|passage=Neben mir wohnt ein Philosoph / Fenster auf, ich hör Türkenmelodien / Ich fühl mich gut / Ich '''steh''' auf Berlin}}

====Usage notes====
The most frequent auxiliary with ''stehen'' is {{m|de|haben}}: ''Ich habe gestanden.'' (NB: This expression also happens to be the perfect tense of {{m|de|gestehen}}: ''I have confessed.'') In northern and central Germany, only this form is used. In southern Germany, Austria, and Switzerland, {{m|de|sein}} is common in the vernacular and also, alternatively, in standard usage: ''Ich bin gestanden.''

